    return 0;
}

editorial:
## The recursion writes itself

A node's depth is 1 plus the deeper of its two subtrees. An empty subtree has depth 0, which is the base case.

```cpp
int depth(TreeNode* node) {
    if (!node) return 0;                 // base case: nothing here
    return 1 + max(depth(node->left), depth(node->right));
}
```

This is a **post-order** traversal: the node's answer depends on its children's answers, so both children must be computed before the node can be. Recognising which traversal order a problem needs is most of tree work — see the traversals lesson.

## The iterative version, and when you need it

Recursion depth equals the tree's height. A degenerate tree — every node having only a left child — has height `n`, so at 10^4 nodes the recursive version is near the edge and at 10^5 it overflows. Level-order with a queue has no such limit:

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

struct TreeNode {
    int val;
    TreeNode* left = nullptr;
    TreeNode* right = nullptr;
    explicit TreeNode(int v) : val(v) {}
};

int main() {
    // ... read_tree() as given in the starter code ...
    TreeNode* root = nullptr;   // replaced by read_tree() in your solution

    int levels = 0;
    if (root) {
        queue<TreeNode*> q;
        q.push(root);
        while (!q.empty()) {
            // Snapshot the size: the loop below pushes the NEXT level onto
            // the same queue, and without this the level boundary is lost.
            int width = (int)q.size();
            for (int i = 0; i < width; ++i) {
                TreeNode* node = q.front();
                q.pop();
                if (node->left) q.push(node->left);
                if (node->right) q.push(node->right);
            }
            ++levels;
        }
    }
    cout << levels << '\n';
}
```

The number of level iterations *is* the depth. That `int width = (int)q.size();` is the line that separates a correct level-order traversal from one that merges every level into a single pass.

## Complexity

| | Time | Space |
|---|---|---|
| Recursive | O(n) | O(h) stack — O(n) worst case |
| Iterative BFS | O(n) | O(w), the widest level |

Both visit every node once. The space differs by shape: BFS is worse on a bushy tree, recursion is worse on a skewed one.

## Pitfalls

- **Returning 0 for a single node.** One node is depth 1; the base case is the *null* child, not the leaf.
- **Counting edges instead of nodes.** This problem counts nodes, so a single node is 1. The "height" convention that counts edges would give 0 — read the statement.
- **Forgetting the empty tree.** Must print 0, and `if (root)` is what guards the BFS.

