editorial:
## Four cases, in order

```cpp
bool same(TreeNode* a, TreeNode* b) {
    if (!a && !b) return true;       // 1. both absent: identical
    if (!a || !b) return false;      // 2. exactly one absent: shapes differ
    if (a->val != b->val) return false;   // 3. values differ
    return same(a->left, b->left)    // 4. recurse on both sides
        && same(a->right, b->right);
}
```

The order matters. Case 1 must come before case 2, or two nulls would be reported as different. Case 2 must come before case 3, or `a->val` would dereference a null pointer.

That ordering is the whole problem, and it is worth noticing that the three checks form a complete decision: after them, both nodes exist and have equal values, so only the children remain in question.

## Why structure matters, not just values

`1 2` (a root with a left child) and `1 null 2` (a root with a right child) hold the same multiset of values and are not the same tree. Case 2 catches it: at the root's left position one tree has a node and the other has null.

This is also why comparing in-order traversals is **not** a valid shortcut — different shapes can produce the same traversal.

## The `&&` is doing real work

`&&` short-circuits, so once the left subtrees differ the right ones are never examined. On two large trees that differ near the root, this returns almost immediately.

## The iterative version

If recursion depth is a concern, walk both trees in lockstep with one stack of pairs:

```cpp
bool same_iter(TreeNode* a, TreeNode* b) {
    stack<pair<TreeNode*, TreeNode*>> st;
    st.push({a, b});
    while (!st.empty()) {
        auto [x, y] = st.top();
        st.pop();
        if (!x && !y) continue;
        if (!x || !y) return false;
        if (x->val != y->val) return false;
        st.push({x->left, y->left});
        st.push({x->right, y->right});
    }
    return true;
}
```

Note `continue` rather than `return true` when both are null — one matching pair of absent subtrees does not prove the whole trees match, only that this branch is fine. Writing `return true` there is a real bug that passes most tests.

## Complexity

O(min(n, m)) time — the traversal stops at the first difference. O(h) space for the stack or recursion.

## Pitfalls

- **Checking values before checking for null.** Segfault.
- **`if (!a && !b) return false;`** — inverted base case.
- **Comparing traversals** instead of walking in lockstep.
- **`return true` instead of `continue`** in the iterative version.
- **Only recursing on one side**, or writing `||` instead of `&&`.

