editorial:
## Three lines

```cpp
TreeNode* invert(TreeNode* node) {
    if (!node) return nullptr;
    swap(node->left, node->right);      // swap the POINTERS at this node
    invert(node->left);
    invert(node->right);
    return node;
}
```

Swapping the two child pointers mirrors this node; recursing mirrors everything below. That is the whole algorithm.

This works as **pre-order** (swap, then recurse) or **post-order** (recurse, then swap) — both are correct, because the swap at a node is independent of what happens inside its subtrees. It is one of the few tree problems where the traversal order genuinely does not matter.

## What must not be swapped

Swap the **child pointers**, not the values. Swapping `node->left->val` with `node->right->val` only exchanges two numbers and leaves the structure untouched — so it gives the right answer on a perfect tree of depth 2 and the wrong one on anything deeper or asymmetric.

## The iterative version

Any traversal works; here it is with a queue:

```cpp
void invert_iter(TreeNode* root) {
    if (!root) return;
    queue<TreeNode*> q;
    q.push(root);
    while (!q.empty()) {
        TreeNode* node = q.front();
        q.pop();
        swap(node->left, node->right);
        if (node->left) q.push(node->left);
        if (node->right) q.push(node->right);
    }
}
```

Push after the swap or before — either is fine, since the swap does not change *which* nodes are children, only their order.

## Dry run on the sample

```
     4                  4
   /   \              /   \
  2     7     -->    7     2
 / \   / \          / \   / \
1   3 6   9        9   6 3   1
```

Level order of the result: `4 7 2 9 6 3 1`. Reading it off confirms the mirror: each level is reversed, which is what a full mirroring looks like on a perfect tree.

## Complexity

O(n) time — every node is visited once and does O(1) work. O(h) space recursively, O(w) iteratively.

## Pitfalls

- **Swapping values instead of pointers.**
- **Forgetting the null base case**, which segfaults at the first leaf.
- **Swapping only at the root.**
- **Reversing each level of the level-order output** instead of inverting the tree. That produces the same string on a *perfect* tree and a different one otherwise — a good reminder to test asymmetric inputs like `1 2`.

