editorial:
## BFS with a level-size snapshot

A plain BFS visits nodes in level order but gives you no idea where one level ends. The fix is one line: record how many nodes are in the queue *before* processing the level, and consume exactly that many.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

struct TreeNode {
    int val;
    TreeNode* left = nullptr;
    TreeNode* right = nullptr;
    explicit TreeNode(int v) : val(v) {}
};

int main() {
    // ... read_tree() as given ...
    TreeNode* root = nullptr;   // replaced by read_tree() in your solution
    if (!root) return 0;        // empty tree prints nothing

    queue<TreeNode*> q;
    q.push(root);

    while (!q.empty()) {
        // 🛑 Snapshot the size. The loop body pushes the NEXT level onto the
        // same queue, so without this the boundary is lost and every level
        // merges into one.
        int width = (int)q.size();

        for (int i = 0; i < width; ++i) {
            TreeNode* node = q.front();
            q.pop();
            cout << node->val;
            if (i + 1 < width) cout << ' ';
            if (node->left) q.push(node->left);
            if (node->right) q.push(node->right);
        }
        cout << '\n';
    }
}
```

## Why the snapshot works

At the top of each outer iteration the queue holds **exactly** the nodes of one level — nothing more, nothing less. That is an invariant, and it is worth stating: the body removes all `width` of them and enqueues precisely their children, which together are exactly the next level. The invariant is restored, so the next iteration is correct for the same reason.

Without the snapshot, `q.size()` grows mid-loop and the condition never lines up with a level boundary.

## The DFS alternative

You can also do this depth-first, passing the depth down and appending to the right bucket:

```cpp
void collect(TreeNode* node, int depth, vector<vector<int>>& levels) {
    if (!node) return;
    // The first node to reach this depth creates its row. Because we recurse
    // left before right, each row fills left to right — which is what makes
    // the output order correct despite this being a depth-first walk.
    if ((int)levels.size() == depth) levels.push_back({});
    levels[depth].push_back(node->val);
    collect(node->left, depth + 1, levels);
    collect(node->right, depth + 1, levels);
}
```

Same O(n), and it needs the whole result in memory rather than streaming it. Useful when the problem wants the levels as data rather than printed.

## Variants built on the same loop

- **Right side view** — print the last value of each level.
- **Zigzag order** — reverse alternate levels.
- **Bottom-up order** — collect into a vector and reverse it.
- **Level averages** — sum each level and divide by `width`.

All four are the snapshot loop with a different line inside, which is why this is the template worth memorising rather than the variants.

## Complexity

O(n) time. O(w) space where `w` is the widest level — up to `n/2` for a perfect tree, which is the worst case for BFS and the best case for recursion depth.

## Pitfalls

- **Omitting the size snapshot.**
- **Pushing null children.** They must be filtered, or the output gains phantom entries.
- **Not handling the empty tree.**
- **Calling `q.size()` inside the inner loop condition.** Same bug as omitting the snapshot, in disguise.

