editorial:
## The wrong answer almost everyone writes first

```cpp
// WRONG. Checks only the immediate children.
bool bad(TreeNode* n) {
    if (!n) return true;
    if (n->left && n->left->val >= n->val) return false;
    if (n->right && n->right->val <= n->val) return false;
    return bad(n->left) && bad(n->right);
}
```

This accepts the sample input `5 1 4 null null 3 6`:

```
    5
   / \
  1   4        <- 4 < 5, so 4 is wrongly in the RIGHT subtree...
     / \          ...and the local check never notices
    3   6
```

Every parent-child pair satisfies the local rule, yet 3 and 4 both sit in 5's right subtree while being smaller than 5. The BST property is about **all descendants**, so a local check cannot decide it.

## The fix: carry a valid range down

Each node inherits an open interval `(lo, hi)` that it must lie within. Descending left tightens the upper bound to the current node; descending right tightens the lower bound.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

struct TreeNode {
    int val;
    TreeNode* left = nullptr;
    TreeNode* right = nullptr;
    explicit TreeNode(int v) : val(v) {}
};

// optional<long long> rather than a sentinel: values span the whole int32
// range, so there is no "impossible" int to use as "no bound", and INT_MIN /
// INT_MAX as sentinels break on a tree containing those very values.
bool valid(TreeNode* node, optional<long long> lo, optional<long long> hi) {
    if (!node) return true;
    if (lo && node->val <= *lo) return false;
    if (hi && node->val >= *hi) return false;
    // The bounds TIGHTEN as we descend, so each node is checked against every
    // ancestor rather than just its parent.
    return valid(node->left, lo, node->val)
        && valid(node->right, node->val, hi);
}

int main() {
    // ... read_tree() as given ...
    TreeNode* root = nullptr;   // replaced by read_tree() in your solution
    cout << (valid(root, nullopt, nullopt) ? "true" : "false") << '\n';
}
```

Trace the failing sample: node 4 is reached as the right child of 5, so it carries `lo = 5`. `4 <= 5`, rejected immediately — the ancestor constraint is what the local check was missing.

## Why not INT_MIN and INT_MAX as sentinels

Values are allowed to be exactly `-2^31` and `2^31 - 1`. A tree whose root is `INT_MIN` would be rejected by `node->val <= INT_MIN`, even though a single node is always a valid BST. `optional` says "no bound" without borrowing a real value to mean it; widening to `long long` is the other common fix.

## Approach B: in-order traversal must be strictly increasing

An in-order walk of a valid BST yields sorted values. So traverse in order and check each value against the previous one:

```cpp
bool check_inorder(TreeNode* node, optional<long long>& prev) {
    if (!node) return true;
    if (!check_inorder(node->left, prev)) return false;
    // Strictly greater: equal values are not allowed in a BST.
    if (prev && node->val <= *prev) return false;
    prev = node->val;
    return check_inorder(node->right, prev);
}
```

This is arguably the more elegant characterisation, and it only needs one previous value rather than two bounds. It also generalises: the "recover a BST with two swapped nodes" problem is built on exactly this observation.

## Complexity

O(n) time for both — every node is visited once. O(h) space for recursion, which is O(n) on a degenerate tree. At 10^4 nodes a fully skewed tree is close to the stack limit; the iterative in-order walk with an explicit stack avoids the risk.

## Pitfalls

- **Checking only parent and child.** The headline bug.
- **`INT_MIN` / `INT_MAX` sentinels** against a tree containing them.
- **Using `<` instead of `<=`** in the bound checks, which would accept duplicates.
- **Collecting the in-order traversal into a vector and checking `is_sorted`.** Correct, but O(n) extra space for no reason — and `is_sorted` permits equal neighbours, so you need `adjacent_find` or a strict comparator.

