editorial:
## The one fact you need

**An in-order traversal of a BST visits values in ascending order.** So the `k`-th node visited in order is the `k`-th smallest. No sorting, no heap.

## Iterative in-order, stopping early

The iterative form is the one to prefer here, because it can stop the moment it has counted `k` nodes rather than walking the whole tree.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

struct TreeNode {
    int val;
    TreeNode* left = nullptr;
    TreeNode* right = nullptr;
    explicit TreeNode(int v) : val(v) {}
};

int main() {
    // ... read_tree() as given ...
    TreeNode* root = nullptr;   // replaced by read_tree() in your solution
    int k = 0;
    cin >> k;

    stack<TreeNode*> st;
    TreeNode* cur = root;
    int seen = 0;

    while (cur || !st.empty()) {
        // Descend as far left as possible, stacking the path. The leftmost
        // node is the smallest, which is where in-order must begin.
        while (cur) {
            st.push(cur);
            cur = cur->left;
        }
        cur = st.top();
        st.pop();

        if (++seen == k) {
            cout << cur->val << '\n';
            return 0;          // stop immediately — no need to finish
        }

        // Having visited this node, its right subtree comes next.
        cur = cur->right;
    }
}
```

The structure is worth internalising: *go left as far as you can, visit, then go right once and repeat.* That is in-order traversal with an explicit stack, and it is the same loop used for "BST iterator" problems.

## The recursive version

```cpp
void walk(TreeNode* node, int k, int& seen, int& answer) {
    if (!node || seen >= k) return;      // the second test is the pruning
    walk(node->left, k, seen, answer);
    if (++seen == k) answer = node->val;
    walk(node->right, k, seen, answer);
}
```

Shorter, but note it needs `seen` and `answer` passed **by reference**. Passing `seen` by value is the classic bug: each call gets its own copy, the count never accumulates across subtrees, and the answer is silently wrong.

The `seen >= k` guard is what stops it exploring the rest of the tree after the answer is found. Without it the complexity is O(n) rather than O(h + k).

## Complexity

| | Time | Space |
|---|---|---|
| In-order, early exit | **O(h + k)** | O(h) |
| Full in-order into a vector | O(n) | O(n) |

For `k = 1` on a balanced tree that is O(log n) — it just walks to the leftmost node.

## The follow-up worth knowing

If the tree is modified often and `k`-th queries are frequent, augment each node with the **size of its subtree**. Then finding the `k`-th smallest is a descent: if the left subtree has `L` nodes, compare `k` with `L + 1` and go left, stop, or go right with `k - L - 1`. That is O(h) per query regardless of `k`, and it is how order-statistic trees work.

## Pitfalls

- **Passing the counter by value.**
- **Collecting everything then indexing.** Works, O(n) space, discards the early exit.
- **Using pre-order or post-order.** Only in-order is sorted.
- **Off-by-one on `k`.** `k = 1` is the smallest, so increment *before* comparing, or compare then increment consistently.

