## The BST property makes this trivial

Start at the root and compare. If **both** targets are smaller than the current node, the answer is somewhere to the left. If both are larger, it is to the right. Otherwise — one on each side, or the current node *is* one of them — this node is the split point and therefore the lowest common ancestor.

No recursion needed, no parent pointers, no path storage:

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

struct TreeNode {
    int val;
    TreeNode* left = nullptr;
    TreeNode* right = nullptr;
    explicit TreeNode(int v) : val(v) {}
};

int main() {
    // ... read_tree() as given ...
    TreeNode* root = nullptr;   // replaced by read_tree() in your solution
    long long p = 0, q = 0;
    cin >> p >> q;

    TreeNode* cur = root;
    while (cur) {
        if (p < cur->val && q < cur->val) {
            cur = cur->left;            // both in the left subtree
        } else if (p > cur->val && q > cur->val) {
            cur = cur->right;           // both in the right subtree
        } else {
            // They split here, or cur is one of them. Either way this is the
            // deepest node with both as descendants — note a node counts as
            // its own descendant, which is why equality lands in this branch.
            cout << cur->val << '\n';
            return 0;
        }
    }
}
```

## Why the split point is the answer

Suppose at node `x` the targets go different ways — `p` left, `q` right. Then `x` has both as descendants, so it is *a* common ancestor. Could anything deeper also be one? No: any deeper node lies entirely within `x`'s left subtree or entirely within its right, and so can contain only one of the two. Therefore `x` is the lowest.

The case where `cur->val` equals `p` is the same argument with the "a node is its own descendant" convention: nothing below `p` can contain `p` itself.

## Note on the input order

The code never assumes `p < q`. Both comparisons are made explicitly against `cur->val`, so `2 8` and `8 2` behave identically. A solution that sorts them first is also fine — but one that assumes the order without checking will fail half the tests.

## Complexity

O(h) time — one root-to-node descent, so O(log n) on a balanced tree. **O(1) space**, which is the payoff for iterating rather than recursing.

## The general-tree version is a different problem

Without the BST ordering you cannot decide which way to go, so the standard solution recurses everywhere and reports upwards:

```cpp
TreeNode* lca(TreeNode* node, TreeNode* p, TreeNode* q) {
    if (!node || node == p || node == q) return node;
    TreeNode* l = lca(node->left, p, q);
    TreeNode* r = lca(node->right, p, q);
    // Found in both subtrees -> this node is the split point.
    if (l && r) return node;
    return l ? l : r;
}
```

That is O(n) time and O(h) space. Worth knowing both, and worth noticing that the BST version is faster precisely because the ordering tells you where *not* to look.

## Pitfalls

- **Assuming `p < q`.**
- **Using `<=` in the descent**, which would walk past a node equal to a target instead of stopping there.
- **Reaching for the general O(n) algorithm** and discarding the ordering that makes the problem easy.

