## Two functions, composed

This is "Same Tree" used as a helper. At every node of the big tree, ask whether the subtree rooted there is *identical* to the target.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

struct TreeNode {
    int val;
    TreeNode* left = nullptr;
    TreeNode* right = nullptr;
    explicit TreeNode(int v) : val(v) {}
};

/** Exactly the Same Tree problem — note the ordering of the null checks. */
bool identical(TreeNode* a, TreeNode* b) {
    if (!a && !b) return true;
    if (!a || !b) return false;
    if (a->val != b->val) return false;
    return identical(a->left, b->left) && identical(a->right, b->right);
}

bool contains(TreeNode* root, TreeNode* sub) {
    // An empty target is a subtree of anything, including an empty tree.
    if (!sub) return true;
    // A non-empty target cannot be found in an empty tree.
    if (!root) return false;
    // Either this node matches, or the target is somewhere below.
    return identical(root, sub)
        || contains(root->left, sub)
        || contains(root->right, sub);
}

int main() {
    // ... read_tree() as given ...
    TreeNode* root = nullptr;   // replaced by read_tree() in your solution
    TreeNode* sub = nullptr;
    cout << (contains(root, sub) ? "true" : "false") << '\n';
}
```

## The distinction that makes this subtle

`identical` requires an **exact** match all the way down. That is what separates "subtree" from "subtree pattern": consider

```
big:  3            target:  4
     / \                   /
    4   5                 1
   / \
  1   2
```

The target `4 1` is *not* a subtree of the big tree, even though the big tree has a node 4 with a left child 1 — because that node also has a right child 2, and a subtree includes all descendants. `identical` catches it: at the right child, one side is null and the other is node 2, so case 2 rejects.

This is the test case people fail, and it is included below as `3 4 5 1 2` against `4 1`.

## The short-circuit is load-bearing

`||` evaluates left to right and stops at the first `true`. So once a match is found, no further subtrees are examined. Similarly `&&` inside `identical` abandons a comparison at the first difference, which is why the average cost is far below the worst case.

## Complexity

O(n · m) worst case — every one of the `n` nodes may trigger an O(m) comparison. With `n = 2000` and `m = 1000` that is 2·10^6, comfortable.

The worst case is real though: a big tree of all identical values against a target of all identical values forces the full comparison at every node.

## The O(n + m) approach

Serialise both trees in pre-order **with explicit null markers**, then ask whether the target's string is a substring of the big tree's. With a linear-time substring search (KMP) the whole thing is O(n + m).

The null markers are essential. Without them, the trees `1 2` and `1 null 2` serialise identically, and unrelated trees produce false matches. Each value also needs a delimiter, or `1,2` and `12` collide — the same ambiguity problem as the anagram signature in Group Anagrams.

## Pitfalls

- **Treating a partial match as a subtree.** The headline bug.
- **Returning `false` for an empty target.**
- **Checking only whether the root values match somewhere**, without comparing structure.
- **Serialising without null markers or delimiters.**

