## The distinction the problem is testing

`search` and `prefix` differ by exactly one thing: whether the node you land on is marked as the **end of a stored word**. Without that flag, inserting `apple` would make `search("app")` return true — which is the bug the sample is designed to catch.

## The implementation

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Trie {
public:
    Trie() : nodes_(1) {}            // node 0 is the root: the empty prefix

    void insert(const string& word) {
        int cur = 0;
        for (char ch : word) {
            int c = ch - 'a';
            if (nodes_[cur].next[c] == -1) {
                nodes_.push_back(Node{});           // may reallocate...
                // ...so re-index nodes_[cur] AFTER the push_back. Holding a
                // Node& across the push would be a dangling reference.
                nodes_[cur].next[c] = (int)nodes_.size() - 1;
            }
            cur = nodes_[cur].next[c];
        }
        nodes_[cur].is_word = true;
    }

    bool search(const string& word) const {
        int node = walk(word);
        return node != -1 && nodes_[node].is_word;   // the flag matters here
    }

    bool starts_with(const string& prefix) const {
        return walk(prefix) != -1;                   // reaching it is enough
    }

private:
    struct Node {
        array<int, 26> next;      // -1 means "no child"
        bool is_word = false;
        Node() { next.fill(-1); }
    };

    /** Index of the node for this string, or -1 if the path breaks. */
    int walk(const string& s) const {
        int cur = 0;
        for (char ch : s) {
            int c = ch - 'a';
            cur = nodes_[cur].next[c];
            if (cur == -1) return -1;
        }
        return cur;
    }

    /*
     * Nodes live in one vector and children are INDICES, not pointers.
     * Deliberate: a vector<Node> reallocates as it grows, which would
     * invalidate every raw Node* in the structure. Indices survive
     * reallocation, and the contiguous storage beats tens of thousands of
     * separate allocations on cache behaviour.
     */
    vector<Node> nodes_;
};

int main() {
    ios_base::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n;
    cin >> n;
    Trie trie;

    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        string op, arg;
        cin >> op >> arg;
        if (op == "insert") trie.insert(arg);
        else if (op == "search") cout << (trie.search(arg) ? "true" : "false") << '\n';
        else cout << (trie.starts_with(arg) ? "true" : "false") << '\n';
    }
}
```

## Indices rather than pointers

This is the detail worth stealing for every tree you build in competitive C++. A `vector<Node>` with `int` children:

- survives reallocation, where raw `Node*` would dangle;
- allocates in a handful of large blocks rather than once per node;
- is trivially copyable and serialisable.

The trap to avoid is writing `Node& cur = nodes_[i];` and then calling `push_back` — the reference may dangle immediately. The code above re-indexes after every growth, and the comment says why.

## Why lookup is independent of the dictionary size

A trie walks one character at a time, so `search` costs **O(L)** in the word's length — not in the number of stored words. A trie holding a million words finds a five-letter word in five steps. A hash set matches that for exact lookup, but cannot answer `prefix` at all without scanning everything.

## Complexity

| Operation | Time | Note |
|---|---|---|
| `insert` | O(L) | Creates at most L nodes |
| `search` | O(L) | Independent of word count |
| `prefix` | O(L) | The query a hash set cannot do |
| Memory | O(total length × 26) worst | The `array<int,26>` is the cost |

That memory column is the real trade. 26 ints per node is 104 bytes, so a sparse trie wastes a lot. For a large or unknown alphabet, swap `array<int,26>` for a small `unordered_map<char,int>` or a sorted vector of pairs.

## Pitfalls

- **No `is_word` flag**, making `search` behave like `prefix`.
- **Raw `Node*` children with `vector<Node>` storage.**
- **Holding a reference across `push_back`.**
- **Assuming lowercase** without validating — `ch - 'a'` on an uppercase letter or digit indexes out of bounds.
